  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "I1MwUE+DfYNtHOdmG5HtCMf52eg=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:32:54.404Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The evidence recorded for Scott Logic is unusually concrete: a permissive open-source policy, a public GitHub organisation, a fifteen-year engineering blog, FINOS membership with the CTO on the board and leading a Technical Steering Committee, and FDC3 interoperability standards work. The verdict \u2014 the strongest documented ethos of any firm researched \u2014 rests on documentation rather than on declared intent.\n\nThe distinction matters because open-source enthusiasm is cheap to claim. Board-level involvement in a foundation and fifteen years of public writing are commitments with a cost and a history, which is what makes them evidence. FDC3 adds a second property: the firm already builds on open interoperability standards, so a component defined by a shared schema is a familiar object rather than an unusual request.\n\nThe firm is also recorded as technology-agnostic, which cuts both ways. There is no incumbent platform occupying the slot a record layer would fill \u2014 the disqualifier that removes several other leads in the corpus \u2014 but there is equally no product line whose gap makes the case automatically. The argument has to be made on merit, to an audience unusually well equipped to evaluate it.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**1 \u00b7 start here**\n- Scott Logic (UK \u00b7 350+)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Strongest documented OSS ethos of any firm researched. FDC3 work means they already build on open interop standards. Stated technology-agnostic.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:32:54.404047Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:32:54.404051Z"
    }
  },
